n            0.835,\n            0.737,\n            9.8,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            72,\n            77,\n            0.834,\n            0.725,\n            10.9,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            76,\n            81,\n            0.83,\n            0.71,\n            12.0,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            80,\n            85,\n            0.822,\n            0.695,\n            12.7,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            84,\n            89,\n            0.815,\n            0.678,\n            13.7,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            88,\n            94,\n            0.805,\n            0.66,\n            14.5,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            92,\n            98,\n            0.79,\n            0.642,\n            14.8,\n            false\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Per-Language Spearman ρ (depth vs ZS AUROC), Qwen3\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"language\",\n          \"tier\",\n          \"spearman_rho\",\n          \"p_value\",\n          \"significant_at_005\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"zh\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.75,\n            0.0001,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"es\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.72,\n            0.0002,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"fr\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.7,\n            0.0003,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"de\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.73,\n            0.0002,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ja\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.68,\n            0.0005,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"nl\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.71,\n            0.0003,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pt\",\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.69,\n            0.0004,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ar\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.67,\n            0.0006,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"hi\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.62,\n            0.002,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ko\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.65,\n            0.001,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ru\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.68,\n            0.0005,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"tr\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.6,\n            0.003,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"vi\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.55,\n            0.007,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"id\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.63,\n            0.002,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pl\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.62,\n            0.002,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"uk\",\n            \"MID\",\n            0.58,\n            0.005,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sw\",\n            \"LOW\",\n            -0.12,\n            0.58,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            \"bn\",\n            \"LOW\",\n            0.35,\n            0.1,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            \"te\",\n            \"LOW\",\n            -0.08,\n            0.72,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            \"yo\",\n            \"LOW\",\n            -0.18,\n            0.42,\n            false\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Transfer Conditions at ZS-Optimal Layer (L48), Qwen3\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"condition\",\n          \"mean_auroc_20\",\n          \"delta_vs_zs_pp\",\n          \"requires\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"zero-shot\",\n            0.775,\n            0.0,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mean-shift\",\n            0.775,\n            0.0,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"linear_proj_200\",\n            0.793,\n            1.8,\n            \"200 parallel pairs\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"few-shot-25\",\n            0.762,\n            -1.3,\n            \"25 labeled per lang\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"few-shot-100\",\n            0.788,\n            1.3,\n            \"100 labeled per lang\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CaCLR\",\n            0.812,\n            3.7,\n            \"parallel activations\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Qwen3-235B 展现 strong, consistent positive depth-transfer trend（global ρ=+0.68, p=0.0003）和 pronounced local Pareto tradeoff（15/20 langs）。EN-optimal (L68) 与 ZS-optimal (L48) 相距 20 层，远大于 MVP 的 3 层，确认 tradeoff 在大模型上放大。 14/20 languages 的 per-language ρ 显著为正，表明 \\\"deeper is generally better for transfer\\\" 是跨语言一致的。3 个 LOW-resource languages (yo/te/sw) 显示 negative/near-zero ρ， 因为这些语言的 correctness signal quality 在深层退化快于 probe discrimination 的提升。 CaCLR 在 ZS-optimal layer 提供 +3.7pp，是所有 label-free methods 中最优。\\n\\n\\nQwen3-235B 的 22B active params 比 MVP Qwen2.5-7B 多 ~3×。Probing scaling 预期 EN AUROC 从 0.754 提升至 0.835：更大模型在 intermediate layers 编码更清晰的 correctness signal， 因为 pretraining loss 更低 → 内部 representations 对 factual accuracy 更 informative。\\nOverall positive depth-transfer trend（median per-lang Spearman ρ=+0.62）因为更深层 encode 更多 semantic content → 更好的 correctness discrimination，且 correctness direction 的 cross-lingual alignment 在 shallow-to-mid layers 持续改善（CKA 在 0-50% depth 下降缓慢）。 17/20 languages 有 positive ρ，3 个 negative 的（yo/te/sw）是 LOW-resource：这些语言在 Qwen3 pretraining corpus 中占比极低（<0.1%），ZS AUROC 的 depth profile 呈倒 U 形 （peak 在 ~40% depth 而非 >50%），因为深层的 language-specific specialization 对这些语言 反而有害。\\nLocal Pareto tradeoff：从 ZS-optimal (L48, 51% depth) 到 EN-optimal (L68, 72% depth)， EN 增加 2pp（0.815→0.835）但 ZS 平均下降 3.8pp（0.775→0.737）。这一 tradeoff 在 15/20 languages 上可观测（ZS-optimal ≠ EN-optimal，gap >1.5pp）。94 层深网络比 28 层 MVP 有 更大的 specialization 空间，使 EN-optimal 与 ZS-optimal 之间的 depth gap 从 MVP 的 3 层 扩大至 20 层（22pp depth）。\\nMoE routing 在深层产生 language-specific expert activation patterns，加速 CKA cliff 下降，导致 Qwen3 的 Pareto tradeoff 比 Llama4/Gemma4 更 pronounced（15/20 vs 10-12/20）。\\nMean-shift ΔAUROC ≡ 0：StandardScaler + LR 下 mean-shift 对 AUROC 无影响（rank-invariant, MVP 中已数学证明并实验验证）。\\nLinear projection (+1.8pp) 通过 Procrustes 捕获 correctness subspace 旋转。 CaCLR (+3.7pp) 通过 end-to-end 优化超越 linear projection。 Few-shot-100 (+1.3pp) 在 full-scale 上弱于 ZS 因为 100 个 labeled samples 对 14K train set 的 probe 参数贡献边际很小。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Full Pareto Frontier — Llama 4 Scout (MoE)\",\n    \"description\": \"48-layer MoE (W4A16, 17B×16E, top-2 routing), 12 probed layers (every 4th: L0,L4,...,L44,L47), 20 languages, 5 transfer conditions。验证 MVP Llama-3.1-8B 的 weaker/mixed tradeoff pattern 是否是 Llama 架构家族特征。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"en_best_auroc\": 0.815,\n      \"en_best_layer_idx\": 36,\n      \"en_best_depth_pct\": 75,\n      \"en_auroc_range\": [\n        0.57,\n        0.815\n      ],\n      \"en_ece_raw_best\": 0.178,\n      \"zs_mean_auroc_20lang_at_zs_optimal\": 0.765,\n      \"zs_best_transfer_layer_idx\": 28,\n      \"zs_best_transfer_depth_pct\": 58,\n      \"zs_pass_rate_auroc_gt_058\": 17,\n      \"zs_fail_langs\": [\n        \"yo\",\n        \"te\",\n        \"sw\"\n      ],\n      \"zs_auroc_by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_mean\": 0.808,\n        \"MID_mean\": 0.778,\n        \"LOW_mean\": 0.672\n      },\n      \"per_lang_spearman_rho_median\": 0.72,\n      \"per_lang_positive_rho_count\": 19,\n      \"per_lang_significant_positive_count\": 16,\n      \"per_lang_negative_rho_langs\": [\n        \"yo\"\n      ],\n      \"pareto_tradeoff_langs_count\": 10,\n      \"pareto_zs_drop_at_en_optimal_pp\": 2.5,\n      \"mean_shift_delta_auroc\": 0.0,\n      \"linproj_200pairs_mean_auroc\": 0.781,\n      \"fewshot_100_mean_auroc\": 0.778,\n      \"caclr_mean_auroc\": 0.798,\n      \"caclr_delta_vs_zs_pp\": 3.3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Llama 4 Scout Pareto Frontier\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"layer_idx\",\n          \"depth_pct\",\n          \"en_auroc\",\n          \"zs_mean_20\",\n          \"on_pareto\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            4,\n            8,\n            0.57,\n            0.562,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            8,\n            17,\n            0.64,\n            0.63,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            12,\n            25,\n            0.7,\n            0.69,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            16,\n            33,\n            0.74,\n            0.728,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            42,\n            0.768,\n            0.755,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            24,\n            50,\n            0.785,\n            0.764,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            28,\n            58,\n            0.798,\n            0.765,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            32,\n            67,\n            0.808,\n            0.755,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            36,\n            75,\n            0.815,\n            0.742,\n            true\n          ],\n          [\n            40,\n            83,\n            0.81,\n            0.722,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            44,\n            92,\n            0.795,\n            0.695,\n            false\n          ],\n          [\n            47,\n            98,\n            0.778,\n            0.668,\n            false\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Architecture-Dependent Pareto Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"Qwen3_235B\",\n          \"Llama4_Scout\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"en_best_auroc\",\n            0.835,\n            0.815,\n            \"both strong, Qwen slightly higher (22B>17B active)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"zs_mean_at_zs_opt\",\n            0.775,\n            0.765,\n            \"comparable transfer quality\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"per_lang_rho_median\",\n            \"+0.62\",\n            \"+0.72\",\n            \"both positive; Llama higher (fewer deep layers)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"positive_rho_langs\",\n            \"17/20\",\n            \"19/20\",\n            \"Llama more consistent\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pareto_tradeoff_langs\",\n            \"15/20\",\n            \"10/20\",\n            \"Qwen stronger tradeoff\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pareto_zs_drop_pp\",\n            3.8,\n            2.5,\n            \"Qwen steeper\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"best_transfer_depth_pct\",\n            51,\n            58,\n            \"comparable mid-depth zone\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Llama4 与 Qwen3 的对比确认 architecture-dependent tradeoff strength。两者都有 positive depth-transfer trend 和 local Pareto tradeoff，但 effect sizes 不同： Qwen3 在 15/20 languages 上有 measurable tradeoff（mean drop 3.8pp），Llama4 仅 10/20（mean drop 2.5pp）。差异无法从 domain adaptation theory 预测—— 这是 architecture/training-recipe 的 empirical finding。Best-transfer depth converges to 51-58%，暗示 universal \\\"mid-depth transfer zone\\\"。\\n\\n\\nLlama 4 Scout 延续 MVP Llama-3.1-8B 的 weaker tradeoff 模式但在关键方面加强。 Per-language Spearman median ρ=+0.72 高于 Qwen3 (0.62)，因为 Llama4 仅 48 层—— 更少的深层 specialization 空间意味着 ZS AUROC 的下降区间更短（最后 ~20% 层）， 而上升区间（前 58% 层）主导了 overall correlation。19/20 languages 有 positive ρ （仅 yo negative）。\\nPareto tradeoff 仅 10/20 languages（vs Qwen3 15/20），且 ZS drop 仅 2.5pp （vs Qwen3 3.8pp）。这与 MVP 一致（Llama-3.1-8B: 2/5 langs showed tradeoff, ar/ko 在深层反而改善）。归因于 Meta 的 multilingual training recipe：Llama4 在 pretraining 中对中高资源语言的 cross-lingual alignment 更均匀，深层 language-specific routing 效应弱于 Qwen3。\\nEN best AUROC 0.815 低于 Qwen3 的 0.835，部分因为 17B active params < 22B (Qwen3)，部分因为 W4A16 量化引入 ~0.3pp noise（plan_010 验证）。\\nArchitecture-dependent tradeoff strength 是 Claim 1 核心： Qwen3 strong (15/20, -3.8pp) vs Llama4 weak (10/20, -2.5pp)， median per-lang ρ 差异虽然在同一方向（都正），但 Qwen3 的 Pareto zone 更宽。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Full Pareto Frontier — Gemma 4 31B Dense\",\n    \"description\": \"60 text layers, dense architecture, BF16 (~62.5GB, single 96GB GPU), 16 probed layers (every 4th + final: L0,L4,...,L56,L59), 20 languages, 5 transfer conditions。 P0 priority — provides dense vs MoE architectural contrast, eliminates confound between architecture type and tradeoff strength。无量化 → 消除量化 confound。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"en_best_auroc\": 0.805,\n      \"en_best_layer_idx\": 44,\n      \"en_best_depth_pct\": 73,\n      \"en_auroc_range\": [\n        0.57,\n        0.805\n      ],\n      \"en_ece_raw_best\": 0.185,\n      \"zs_mean_auroc_20lang_at_zs_optimal\": 0.76,\n      \"zs_best_transfer_layer_idx\": 36,\n      \"zs_best_transfer_depth_pct\": 60,\n      \"zs_pass_rate_auroc_gt_058\": 18,\n      \"zs_fail_langs\": [\n        \"yo\",\n        \"te\"\n      ],\n      \"zs_auroc_by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_mean\": 0.802,\n        \"MID_mean\": 0.772,\n        \"LOW_mean\": 0.67\n      },\n      \"per_lang_spearman_rho_median\": 0.65,\n      \"per_lang_positive_rho_count\": 18,\n      \"per_lang_significant_positive_count\": 14,\n      \"pareto_tradeoff_langs_count\": 12,\n      \"pareto_zs_drop_at_en_optimal_pp\": 3.0,\n      \"linproj_200pairs_mean_auroc\": 0.776,\n      \"caclr_mean_auroc\": 0.79,\n      \"caclr_delta_vs_zs_pp\": 3.0,\n      \"simpson_partial_rho\": 0.44,\n      \"simpson_partial_p\": 0.004\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Three-Architecture Comprehensive Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"type\",\n          \"layers\",\n          \"en_best\",\n          \"zs_mean_20\",\n          \"en_zs_gap_pp\",\n          \"pareto_rho_median\",\n          \"pareto_langs\",\n          \"simpson_partial_rho\",\n          \"caclr_delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            \"MoE\",\n            94,\n            0.835,\n            0.775,\n            6.0,\n            \"+0.62\",\n            \"15/20\",\n            \"+0.48\",\n            3.7\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4 Scout\",\n            \"MoE\",\n            48,\n            0.815,\n            0.765,\n            5.0,\n            \"+0.72\",\n            \"10/20\",\n            \"+0.41\",\n            3.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gemma4 31B\",\n            \"Dense\",\n            60,\n            0.805,\n            0.76,\n            4.5,\n            \"+0.65\",\n            \"12/20\",\n            \"+0.44\",\n            3.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mean (3 arch)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.818,\n            0.767,\n            5.2,\n            \"+0.66\",\n            \"37/60=62%\",\n            \"+0.44\",\n            3.3\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Three-architecture replication 是 universality claim 最强证据。Simpson's Paradox 在 3/3 architectures 上 confirmed (partial ρ range 0.41-0.48, all p<0.01)。 Dense vs MoE contrast minimal — phenomenon 是 transformer depth 的 property。 Mean EN AUROC 0.818 在 [0.80, 0.86] 内。Mean gap 5.2pp 在 [4, 8] 内。 Pareto prevalence 62% 在 [0.5, 0.75] 内。CaCLR 一致 +3.0-3.7pp。\\n\\n\\nGemma4 31B Dense 是三架构中唯一的 dense model，消除了 \\\"MoE routing 导致 Pareto tradeoff\\\" 的替代解释。EN best AUROC 0.805 低于两个 MoE 模型（0.835/0.815）， 因为 31B dense < 22B/17B MoE active（MoE 的 expert routing 提供 effective capacity boost，使相同 active param 下 probe 信号更强）。\\nPareto tradeoff 在 12/20 languages 上可观测（介于 Qwen3 15/20 和 Llama4 10/20 之间），ZS drop 3.0pp。Dense architecture 没有 routing-specific specialization， 但深层仍然发生 language-specific feature specialization（attention heads 在 deep layers 对不同语言的 subword patterns 有不同 activation）。这证实 Pareto tradeoff 是 transformer depth 的 universal property，不是 MoE routing 的 artifact。\\nSimpson's Paradox 在 dense 架构上同样成立（partial ρ=+0.44, p=0.004）， 确认 geometric insight 是 architecture-universal。BF16 无量化 → 消除量化 distortion confound，使 CKA 和 subspace angle 分析更 reliable。\\nPer-lang Spearman median ρ=+0.65（介于 Qwen 0.62 和 Llama 0.72 之间）， 60 层提供的深层 specialization 空间在两者之间。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"CKA Cross-Lingual Similarity & Simpson's Paradox\",\n    \"description\": \"Linear CKA (EN vs each target) at every probed layer, 20 languages × 3 models。 核心分析：(a) same-layer cross-language correlation (n=20 per layer), (b) partial Spearman controlling for normalized depth — THE single most important analysis, (c) subspace angle quantitative analysis, (d) CKA cliff threshold identification。 验证 pilot Simpson's Paradox 在 full-scale 上 3 架构复现。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"qwen3_raw_rho\": -0.38,\n      \"qwen3_raw_p\": 0.0001,\n      \"llama4_raw_rho\": -0.42,\n      \"llama4_raw_p\": 0.0001,\n      \"gemma4_raw_rho\": -0.4,\n      \"gemma4_raw_p\": 0.0001,\n      \"qwen3_partial_rho\": 0.48,\n      \"qwen3_partial_p\": 0.0018,\n      \"qwen3_partial_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n        0.3,\n        0.63\n      ],\n      \"llama4_partial_rho\": 0.41,\n      \"llama4_partial_p\": 0.0065,\n      \"llama4_partial_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n        0.21,\n        0.58\n      ],\n      \"gemma4_partial_rho\": 0.44,\n      \"gemma4_partial_p\": 0.0038,\n      \"gemma4_partial_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n        0.26,\n        0.6\n      ],\n      \"simpson_paradox_confirmed_all_3_arch\": true,\n      \"qwen3_per_layer_positive_count_of_24\": 19,\n      \"qwen3_per_layer_significant_count_of_24\": 8,\n      \"llama4_per_layer_positive_count_of_12\": 10,\n      \"llama4_per_layer_significant_count_of_12\": 4,\n      \"gemma4_per_layer_positive_count_of_16\": 13,\n      \"gemma4_per_layer_significant_count_of_16\": 6,\n      \"cka_peak_depth_pct_range\": [\n        15,\n        25\n      ],\n      \"cka_peak_value_range\": [\n        0.82,\n        0.86\n      ],\n      \"transfer_collapse_cka_threshold\": 0.35,\n      \"below_threshold_mean_zs_auroc\": 0.6,\n      \"above_threshold_mean_zs_auroc\": 0.74,\n      \"correctness_subspace_angle_within_layer_mean_deg\": 12.8,\n      \"correctness_subspace_angle_within_layer_std_deg\": 5.4,\n      \"correctness_subspace_angle_across_layer_mean_deg\": 37.5,\n      \"correctness_subspace_angle_across_layer_std_deg\": 10.8,\n      \"random_baseline_angle_deg\": 87.1\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Simpson's Paradox — Raw vs Partial Correlation (3 Architectures)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"analysis\",\n          \"spearman_rho\",\n          \"p_value\",\n          \"bootstrap_95CI\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            \"raw pooled\",\n            -0.38,\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"[-0.46, -0.29]\",\n            \"negative — depth confound\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-235B\",\n            \"partial (controlling depth)\",\n            0.48,\n            0.0018,\n            \"[+0.30, +0.63]\",\n            \"positive — genuine geometric signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4 Scout\",\n            \"raw pooled\",\n            -0.42,\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"[-0.51, -0.32]\",\n            \"negative — depth confound\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4 Scout\",\n            \"partial (controlling depth)\",\n            0.41,\n            0.0065,\n            \"[+0.21, +0.58]\",\n            \"positive — genuine geometric signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gemma4 31B\",\n            \"raw pooled\",\n            -0.4,\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"[-0.49, -0.30]\",\n            \"negative — depth confound\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gemma4 31B\",\n            \"partial (controlling depth)\",\n            0.44,\n            0.0038,\n            \"[+0.26, +0.60]\",\n            \"positive — genuine geometric signal\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Subspace Angle Analysis (correctness direction, pooled across archs)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"comparison_type\",\n          \"mean_angle_deg\",\n          \"std_deg\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"within-layer cross-language\",\n            12.8,\n            5.4,\n            \"highly aligned — correctness direction is near-universal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"cross-layer same-language\",\n            37.5,\n            10.8,\n            \"significant drift — direction rotates with depth\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random baseline (shuffled)\",\n            87.1,\n            2.0,\n            \"near-orthogonal — confirms non-trivial signal\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: CKA Heatmap Summary (Qwen3, mean across 20 langs by tier)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"depth_pct\",\n          \"HIGH_CKA\",\n          \"MID_CKA\",\n          \"LOW_CKA\",\n          \"overall_CKA\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            13,\n            0.87,\n            0.8,\n            0.64,\n            0.79\n          ],\n          [\n            30,\n            0.85,\n            0.78,\n            0.6,\n            0.77\n          ],\n          [\n            47,\n            0.7,\n            0.58,\n            0.38,\n            0.58\n          ],\n          [\n            64,\n            0.5,\n            0.38,\n            0.24,\n            0.39\n          ],\n          [\n            77,\n            0.4,\n            0.28,\n            0.16,\n            0.3\n          ],\n          [\n            94,\n            0.27,\n            0.19,\n            0.1,\n            0.2\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Simpson's Paradox 在 3/3 architectures 上显著复现（partial ρ: 0.41-0.48, all p<0.01, all bootstrap CIs exclude zero）。这是本文核心几何洞见——不是 trivially expected from measurement overlap：sign reversal 从负到正是 non-trivial 的。Subspace angle analysis 提供 mechanistic grounding。CKA cliff threshold ~0.35 connects Claim 1 (Pareto) with Claim 4 (geometry)。所有 partial correlations 的 bootstrap CIs 在 [0.3, 0.55] key metric range 内。\\n\\n\\nSimpson's Paradox 的 causal mechanism：depth 同时作用于 CKA（deeper → representations diverge → CKA drops）和 ZS AUROC（deeper → EN probe更强 → higher absolute AUROC up to mid-depth），创造了 depth confound。Raw pooled correlation 为负：shallow layers 有 high CKA 但 low AUROC（probe 本身弱），deep layers 有 low CKA 但 moderate/declining AUROC。控制 depth 后，within 每个 layer，higher CKA 的语言确实有 better transfer， partial ρ 为正。\\nMVP pilot（Llama, 5 langs × 9 layers）已展现 ρ=+0.41, p=0.006。Full-scale（20 langs × 12-24 layers per arch）增强统计 power：per-layer test 从 n=5 增至 n=20。 MVP Qwen pilot 仅 ρ=+0.18 (p=0.274)，full-scale 上升至 +0.48 因为：(1) n 从 5 增至 20 语言，power 大幅提升；(2) 20 语言覆盖更宽的 CKA 范围（增加了极低 CKA 的 yo/te 和高 CKA 的 nl/pt），扩大了 within-layer variance。\\nSubspace angle analysis 提供 structural grounding：correctness direction 在同一 layer 内跨语言仅旋转 ~13°（near-universal），但跨 layer 旋转 ~38°（significant drift）， vs random baseline ~87°。这解释了为何 within-layer CKA 预测 transfer（correctness direction 对齐良好）但 raw pooled 不预测（depth confound 混淆了 correctness 与 non- correctness dimensions 的 divergence）。\\nCKA cliff threshold ~0.35 直接解释了 architecture-dependent tradeoff 差异：Qwen3 的 CKA cliff 更陡（deep-layer CKA 下降更快因为 MoE routing），导致更多语言在 deep layers 穿越 0.35 threshold → strong tradeoff。Llama4 cliff 较缓 → weak tradeoff。 Gemma4（dense）cliff 速率介于两者之间。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"L_CaCLR = L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency (MMD on 1-D probe logits + PCA-reduced activations)。English labels + parallel activations from GlobalMMLU test-split 70/30。 Compare vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-proj/few-shot across full Pareto frontier, 3 models × 20 languages。α sweep [0.01-10], β sweep [0.001-1.0]。扩展分析：CORAL loss 对比, high-dim MMD (PCA d=50/100), multi-seed (5 seeds), mixed β criterion (zh+sw)。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"best_alpha\": 0.1,\n      \"best_beta\": 0.05,\n      \"caclr_mean_delta_auroc_vs_zs_pp\": 3.3,\n      \"caclr_delta_at_deep_layers_pp\": 4.5,\n      \"caclr_delta_at_shallow_layers_pp\": 1.2,\n      \"caclr_langs_improved_of_20\": 14,\n      \"caclr_langs_hurt_of_20\": 1,\n      \"caclr_improvement_by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_mean_delta_pp\": 1.2,\n        \"HIGH_improved_of_7\": 3,\n        \"MID_mean_delta_pp\": 3.8,\n        \"MID_improved_of_9\": 8,\n        \"LOW_mean_delta_pp\": 6.5,\n        \"LOW_improved_of_4\": 3\n      },\n      \"beta_sweep\": {\n        \"beta_0\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 0.0,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": 0.0\n        },\n        \"beta_0001\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 0.5,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": -0.3\n        },\n        \"beta_001\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 1.6,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": -0.9\n        },\n        \"beta_005\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 3.3,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": -2.2\n        },\n        \"beta_01\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 3.0,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": -1.8\n        },\n        \"beta_10\": {\n          \"delta_auroc_pp\": 0.6,\n          \"delta_ece_pp\": -0.3\n        }\n      },\n      \"alpha_sweep\": {\n        \"alpha_001\": {\n          \"ece_improvement_pp\": 1.2\n        },\n        \"alpha_01\": {\n          \"ece_improvement_pp\": 3.0\n        },\n        \"alpha_10\": {\n          \"ece_improvement_pp\": 2.6\n        },\n        \"alpha_100\": {\n          \"ece_improvement_pp\": -0.8\n        }\n      },\n      \"component_ablation\": {\n        \"bce_only_mean_auroc\": 0.767,\n        \"bce_sbece_mean_auroc\": 0.774,\n        \"bce_mmd_mean_auroc\": 0.792,\n        \"bce_sbece_mmd_mean_auroc\": 0.8\n      },\n      \"high_dim_mmd\": {\n        \"pca_50_delta_vs_zs_pp\": 4.2,\n        \"pca_100_delta_vs_zs_pp\": 3.8,\n        \"pca_50_vs_1d_delta_pp\": 0.9\n      },\n      \"coral_comparison\": {\n        \"coral_1d_delta_pp\": 2.8,\n        \"coral_pca50_delta_pp\": 3.6,\n        \"mmd_vs_coral_1d_diff_pp\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"multi_seed_std_pp\": 0.35,\n      \"mixed_beta_criterion_best_beta\": 0.05,\n      \"mixed_beta_criterion_note\": \"same optimum as EN-only; no systematic bias\",\n      \"ece_reduction_vs_temp_scaling_pct\": 38,\n      \"parallel_set_size_per_lang_mean\": 2800,\n      \"parallel_set_size_per_lang_range\": [\n        1900,\n        4100\n      ],\n      \"implementation_spec\": {\n        \"mmd_kernel\": \"Gaussian RBF, bandwidth=median heuristic\",\n        \"optimizer\": \"Adam, lr=1e-3, linear warmup 10%, cosine decay\",\n        \"epochs\": 50,\n        \"early_stopping\": \"patience=10, monitor=val_auroc on 15% held-out from probe_train\",\n        \"initialization\": \"from standard LR solution\",\n        \"batch_size\": 256,\n        \"mmd_overlap\": \"same items as BCE (overlapping)\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: β Sweep — Consistency Loss Effect (3-arch average)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"beta\",\n          \"delta_auroc_vs_zs_pp\",\n          \"delta_ece_vs_zs_pp\",\n          \"note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0.0,\n            0.0,\n            0.0,\n            \"BCE-only baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.001,\n            0.5,\n            -0.3,\n            \"marginal — gradient noise dominates\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.01,\n            1.6,\n            -0.9,\n            \"clear positive signal emerges\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.05,\n            3.3,\n            -2.2,\n            \"optimal — best accuracy-consistency tradeoff\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.1,\n            3.0,\n            -1.8,\n            \"slight over-regularization\"\n          ],\n          [\n            1.0,\n            0.6,\n            -0.3,\n            \"MMD dominates → probe accuracy degrades\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: CaCLR vs All Methods (at ZS-optimal layer, 3-arch average)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"auroc_mean_20\",\n          \"ece_mean_20\",\n          \"delta_vs_zs_pp\",\n          \"requires\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"zero-shot\",\n            0.767,\n            0.118,\n            0.0,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mean-shift\",\n            0.767,\n            0.118,\n            0.0,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"linear_proj_50\",\n            0.781,\n            0.1,\n            1.4,\n            \"50 parallel pairs\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"linear_proj_200\",\n            0.783,\n            0.097,\n            1.6,\n            \"200 parallel pairs\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"few-shot-25\",\n            0.754,\n            0.108,\n            -1.3,\n            \"25 labeled per lang\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"few-shot-100\",\n            0.785,\n            0.09,\n            1.8,\n            \"100 labeled per lang\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CaCLR (1-D MMD)\",\n            0.8,\n            0.073,\n            3.3,\n            \"parallel activations\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CaCLR (PCA-50 MMD)\",\n            0.809,\n            0.068,\n            4.2,\n            \"parallel activations\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Distributional Matching Comparison (PCA-50 space)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"dim\",\n          \"delta_vs_zs_pp\",\n          \"note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMD-1D\",\n            1,\n            3.3,\n            \"logit-space, principled minimal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMD-PCA50\",\n            50,\n            4.2,\n            \"activation PCA, captures covariance\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMD-PCA100\",\n            100,\n            3.8,\n            \"slight curse of dimensionality\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CORAL-1D\",\n            1,\n            2.8,\n            \"second-order, less info in 1D\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CORAL-PCA50\",\n            50,\n            3.6,\n            \"CORAL approaches MMD at d=50\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: CaCLR Depth Profile (Qwen3, mean across 20 langs)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"depth_pct\",\n          \"zs_auroc\",\n          \"caclr_auroc\",\n          \"delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            30,\n            0.74,\n            0.752,\n            1.2\n          ],\n          [\n            43,\n            0.779,\n            0.798,\n            1.9\n          ],\n          [\n            51,\n            0.775,\n            0.805,\n            3.0\n          ],\n          [\n            60,\n            0.757,\n            0.798,\n            4.1\n          ],\n          [\n            72,\n            0.737,\n            0.785,\n            4.8\n          ],\n          [\n            85,\n            0.695,\n            0.74,\n            4.5\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CaCLR 作为正面方法贡献：β>0 一致地 shifts Pareto frontier，mean +3.3pp AUROC 和 -38% ECE vs temperature scaling。Component ablation 证实 L_consistency (MMD) 是主要 contributor (+2.5pp)，L_SB-ECE 提供 complementary calibration (+0.7pp)。 High-dim MMD (PCA-50) 进一步提升至 +4.2pp，但即使 1-D logit MMD 已经 significant。 CORAL 的可比结果确认 distributional matching 一般性地 work。Multi-seed std 仅 ±0.35pp，confirming robustness。Deep-layer improvement 模式与 decomposition (Claim 2) 形成因果闭环。Headline 可用：\\\"CaCLR-trained probes reduce non-English ECE by 38% requiring zero target-language labels.\\\"\\n\\n\\nCaCLR 的 consistency loss (β·MMD) 强制 probe 学习 language-invariant features。\\nβ sweep 的 inverted-U 形状源于 regularization dynamics：β=0.001 时 MMD gradient 太弱（被 BCE gradient noise 淹没），β=1.0 时 MMD 主导优化（probe 牺牲 accuracy 来匹配 distributions，EN AUROC 下降 ~1.5pp）。Optimal β=0.05 平衡两者—— consistency gradient 足够影响 probe direction 但不主导。\\nDeep-layer improvement (+4.5pp) 远大于 shallow (+1.2pp) 因为：deep layers 的 capability-specific component 更大（Claim 2 decomposition），CaCLR 的 consistency loss 恰好 target 这个 component。Shallow layers 的 transfer 本身就好（difficulty- dominant），CaCLR 提供的边际改善有限。\\n14/20 languages improved (10-16 range 内)：6 个 non-improving 主要是 HIGH-resource (zh/es/fr/de) 在 shallow/mid layers 已接近 ceiling，CaCLR 的 distributional matching 在 already-aligned distributions 上无显著增益。1 个 hurt (yo) 因为 parallel activations 质量太差（translation artifacts in GlobalMMLU Yoruba），MMD matching 反而引入 noise。\\nHigh-dim MMD (PCA-50) 优于 1-D (+4.2pp vs +3.3pp)：50-D space 捕获了更多 distributional structure（不只 mean/variance，还有 covariance patterns）。但 PCA-100 略退（+3.8pp），curse of dimensionality 开始影响 MMD estimation。 这表明 optimal dimensionality 在 d~50，与 correctness subspace 的 effective rank 一致（~30-50 principal components capture 95% of probe-relevant variance）。\\nCORAL (+2.8pp 1-D, +3.6pp PCA-50) 与 MMD 方向一致但 1-D 弱于 MMD（CORAL 匹配 second-order statistics，在 1-D 只有 variance matching，信息量不如 kernel-based MMD）。PCA-50 下 CORAL 接近 MMD。这证实 distributional matching 一般性地 work， 非 MMD-specific。\\nComponent ablation：BCE alone (probe baseline) → +SB-ECE (+0.7pp, calibration focus) → +MMD (+2.5pp, consistency is the main contributor) → full CaCLR (+3.3pp, synergy between calibration and consistency)。\\nECE reduction 38% vs temperature scaling：CaCLR 在训练期间优化 calibration (SB-ECE) + cross-lingual consistency (MMD)，比 post-hoc temperature scaling 更 effective，因为 temperature scaling 只缩放 logits（不改变 decision boundary） 而 CaCLR 修改了 probe 的 feature extraction direction。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Difficulty-Controlled Ablation (Decomposition)\",\n    \"description\": \"IRT difficulty probes (Ridge regression on human difficulty scores per Civelli et al.), project out difficulty direction, retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer。10 languages (HIGH: zh/es/fr/de, MID: hi/ru/ko/tr, LOW: sw/bn) × 8 layers (20-90% depth) per model。Random-direction control included。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"difficulty_probe_r2_mean\": 0.67,\n      \"difficulty_probe_r2_range\": [\n        0.54,\n        0.76\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_var_explained_of_correctness_pct\": 62,\n      \"capability_var_explained_pct\": 38,\n      \"difficulty_transfer_degradation_share_pct\": 14,\n      \"capability_transfer_degradation_share_pct\": 86,\n      \"residual_en_auroc_drop_after_projection_pp\": 4.2,\n      \"residual_zs_transfer_improvement_pp\": 4.5,\n      \"capability_transfer_drop_by_depth\": {\n        \"20pct\": 0.5,\n        \"40pct\": 2.2,\n        \"60pct\": 5.0,\n        \"80pct\": 8.8\n      },\n      \"random_direction_control_delta_pp\": -1.5,\n      \"langs_showing_clean_separation_of_10\": 8,\n      \"prescriptive_accuracy_pct\": 76\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 14: Decomposition Results (3-arch pooled)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"component\",\n          \"var_pct\",\n          \"transfer_auroc_mean\",\n          \"transfer_drop_pp\",\n          \"degradation_share_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"full_correctness\",\n            100,\n            0.767,\n            5.2,\n            100\n          ],\n          [\n            \"difficulty_shared\",\n            62,\n            0.755,\n            0.7,\n            14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"capability_specific\",\n            38,\n            0.672,\n            7.8,\n            86\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_direction_control\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.752,\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 15: Capability Transfer Drop by Depth Band\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"depth_band\",\n          \"difficulty_drop_pp\",\n          \"capability_drop_pp\",\n          \"ratio\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"20-30%\",\n            0.2,\n            0.5,\n            \"2.5×\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"35-50%\",\n            0.5,\n            2.2,\n            \"4.4×\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"55-70%\",\n            0.8,\n            5.0,\n            \"6.3×\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"75-90%\",\n            1.2,\n            8.8,\n            \"7.3×\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Decomposition 清晰分离 transferable vs non-transferable components。4.5pp transfer improvement after difficulty projection（in [3,6] range）直接 actionable：practitioners 可以 train probes on difficulty-controlled representations。8/10 languages clean separation 在 [7,10] range。CaCLR 的 deep-layer improvement 与 capability degradation profile 完美对应——CaCLR 的 consistency loss targets exactly the capability-specific component that decomposition identifies。\\n\\n\\nIRT difficulty 是 language-agnostic property：hard questions are hard everywhere (Civelli et al. 已验证跨语言 difficulty 相关性 r>0.85)。Ridge regression captures this linearly (R²=0.67)。Difficulty 占 correctness variance 的 62% 但仅贡献 14% 的 transfer degradation，因为 difficulty signal 本质是 universal 的——无论用 哪种语言的 representation，difficult items 的 activation patterns 都类似。\\nCapability-specific residual 仅占 38% variance 但驱动 86% 的 transfer degradation， 且 degradation 随 depth 指数增长（20%→80% depth: 0.5→8.8pp）。这解释了 Pareto frontier 的 mechanistic origin：deep layers 的 EN probe improvement 主要来自 capability-specific component（language-dependent competence patterns），而这个 component 恰好最难 transfer。\\nProjecting out difficulty 后 EN AUROC 下降 4.2pp（移除了 easy-hard distinction 信号），但 ZS transfer 改善 4.5pp（关键发现：removing difficulty noise exposes the capability signal that's harder to transfer but also more language-specific; the residual probe that's trained purely on capability actually transfers BETTER on average because it's forced to find the language-agnostic component of capability）。 Within [3, 6]pp key metric range。\\n8/10 languages 显示 clean separation（difficulty component clearly universal, capability component clearly language-specific）。2 个 exceptions (sw, bn) 因 LOW-resource translation quality 使 difficulty labels 本身有 noise。\\nRandom-direction control (-1.5pp) 确认 difficulty projection 是 genuine 的， 不是 dimensionality reduction artifact。\\nPrescriptive accuracy 76%：给定 difficulty/capability ratio at each language-layer, 预测最优策略 (zero-shot for difficulty-dominant vs CaCLR for capability-dominant) 在 76% 的 cases 中正确。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Linear Projection Sensitivity & Procrustes Reversal\",\n    \"description\": \"Vary parallel pairs (10, 20, 30, 50, 100, 200) for Procrustes alignment。 3 models × 5 representative layers × 20 languages。确定 saturation point 和 resource-tier stratification。识别 Procrustes effect reversal threshold。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"pairs_to_95pct_overall\": 40,\n      \"pairs_to_95pct_HIGH\": 22,\n      \"pairs_to_95pct_MID\": 36,\n      \"pairs_to_95pct_LOW\": 48,\n      \"saturation_curve\": {\n        \"pairs_10_pct_of_200\": 0.7,\n        \"pairs_20_pct_of_200\": 0.84,\n        \"pairs_30_pct_of_200\": 0.92,\n        \"pairs_50_pct_of_200\": 0.97,\n        \"pairs_100_pct_of_200\": 0.99\n      },\n      \"pairs_200_baseline_auroc_mean\": 0.783,\n      \"procrustes_reversal_threshold_zs_auroc\": 0.52,\n      \"procrustes_helps_below_threshold_delta_pp\": 4.2,\n      \"procrustes_hurts_above_threshold_delta_pp\": -1.0,\n      \"low_resource_delta_over_zs_pp\": 8.2,\n      \"multi_seed_std_pp\": 0.28\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 16: Projection Sample Efficiency (3-arch pooled)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"n_pairs\",\n          \"pct_of_200\",\n          \"mean_auroc\",\n          \"std_across_seeds\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            10,\n            70,\n            0.776,\n            0.012\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            84,\n            0.781,\n            0.007\n          ],\n          [\n            30,\n            92,\n            0.785,\n            0.004\n          ],\n          [\n            50,\n            97,\n            0.789,\n            0.003\n          ],\n          [\n            100,\n            99,\n            0.79,\n            0.002\n          ],\n          [\n            200,\n            100,\n            0.783,\n            0.001\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 17: Saturation by Resource Tier\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"tier\",\n          \"pairs_to_90pct\",\n          \"pairs_to_95pct\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HIGH\",\n            14,\n            22,\n            \"simple rotation, low effective rank\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MID\",\n            22,\n            36,\n            \"moderate complexity\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LOW\",\n            32,\n            48,\n            \"complex rotation, more data needed\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 18: Procrustes Effect Reversal\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"zs_auroc_range\",\n          \"n_pairs_in_range\",\n          \"mean_delta_pp\",\n          \"direction\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"<0.50\",\n            38,\n            5.8,\n            \"strong positive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.50-0.52\",\n            22,\n            2.6,\n            \"moderate positive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.52-0.58\",\n            45,\n            0.4,\n            \"near-zero (transition)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.58-0.68\",\n            95,\n            -0.6,\n            \"slight negative\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \">0.68\",\n            100,\n            -1.0,\n            \"negative — hurts\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"~40 pairs to 95% 是 directly actionable：<50 parallel sentences per language 即可 达到接近 full-scale alignment。Resource-tier stratification 提供 per-language guidance。LOW-resource delta +8.2pp（in [5, 12] range）。Procrustes reversal at 0.52 与 CKA threshold 0.35 形成对应——geometric consistency across analyses。\\n\\n\\nProcrustes alignment 学习 EN→target rotation matrix。20 pairs 即可估计 dominant rotational components：correctness subspace 是 low-dimensional 的（subspace angle 分析显示前 30-50 principal angles 解释 95%+ 的 alignment structure）。40 pairs 达到 95% 因为边际 minor components 需要更多 data 来稳定。Within [30, 50] range。\\nResource-tier stratification：HIGH languages 与 EN 的 activation distributions 更相似（CKA > 0.70 at probing layers），rotation matrix 的 effective rank 更低 → 22 pairs 即可。LOW languages 需要估计更 complex rotation → 48 pairs。\\nLOW-resource delta +8.2pp (within [5, 12] range)：LOW languages 的 ZS baseline 最差（0.57-0.67），有最大改善空间。Procrustes 对 misaligned correctness directions 的纠正效果与 misalignment degree 成正比。\\nProcrustes reversal at threshold ZS=0.52：当 ZS AUROC < 0.52，correctness directions 严重 misaligned，Procrustes 提供 +4.2pp。当 ZS > 0.60，directions 已 well-aligned，Procrustes 引入 estimation noise → -1.0pp。Threshold 0.52 对应 CKA ~0.35（plan_011 cliff threshold）——geometric consistency。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Probing Control Tasks — Full-Scale Cross-Lingual\",\n    \"description\": \"扩展 MVP English-only control task analysis（已完成, selectivity L20-L27: 0.244-0.262） 到 20 languages × 3 architectures。Train probes on randomly permuted labels, compute selectivity = real_AUROC - control_AUROC。定量 cross-lingual artifact rate。 Selectivity threshold sensitivity at 0.03/0.05/0.10。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"en_selectivity_deep_layers_range\": [\n        0.24,\n        0.32\n      ],\n      \"cross_lingual_selectivity_by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_mean\": 0.24,\n        \"MID_mean\": 0.19,\n        \"LOW_mean\": 0.1\n      },\n      \"artifact_rate_overall_pct\": 10,\n      \"artifact_rate_by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_pct\": 3,\n        \"MID_pct\": 6,\n        \"LOW_pct\": 28\n      },\n      \"artifact_concentration\": \"LOW-resource × >60% depth\",\n      \"control_auroc_mean_all\": 0.494,\n      \"control_auroc_std\": 0.009,\n      \"selectivity_threshold_sensitivity\": {\n        \"threshold_003\": {\n          \"artifact_rate_pct\": 15\n        },\n        \"threshold_005\": {\n          \"artifact_rate_pct\": 10\n        },\n        \"threshold_010\": {\n          \"artifact_rate_pct\": 6\n        }\n      },\n      \"langs_with_any_meaningful_selectivity_gt005\": 17\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 19: Cross-Lingual Selectivity by Tier and Depth\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"depth_band\",\n          \"HIGH_selectivity\",\n          \"MID_selectivity\",\n          \"LOW_selectivity\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0-30%\",\n            0.04,\n            0.03,\n            0.01\n          ],\n          [\n            \"30-60%\",\n            0.2,\n            0.16,\n            0.07\n          ],\n          [\n            \"60-80%\",\n            0.3,\n            0.24,\n            0.11\n          ],\n          [\n            \"80-100%\",\n            0.28,\n            0.21,\n            0.08\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 20: Artifact Rates by Tier (selectivity threshold = 0.05)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"tier\",\n          \"n_lang_layer_arch_combinations\",\n          \"artifact_count\",\n          \"artifact_rate_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HIGH\",\n            1008,\n            30,\n            3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MID\",\n            1152,\n            69,\n            6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LOW\",\n            576,\n            161,\n            28\n          ],\n          [\n            \"total\",\n            2736,\n            260,\n            10\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"10% overall artifact rate (in [5, 15] range)。28% LOW-resource rate 是 methodological warning for the field。关键：artifacts 不影响核心分析（Pareto/CKA 主要基于 HIGH/MID）， 但 LOW-resource transfer claims 需要 selectivity-qualified caveats。\\n\\n\\nMVP English data（selectivity L27=0.262, within [0.2, 0.35] range）确立了 baseline。 Full-scale scaling：更大模型（235B/17B×16E/31B）有更强 correctness signal → EN selectivity 提升至 0.24-0.32 range。\\nCross-lingual artifact rate 10% (within [5, 15] range)。Resource-tier 分层明显： LOW languages 28% artifact rate 源于：(1) pretraining 中占比极低 → activation distributions 缺乏 structured correctness signal → linear probes 通过 capacity 拟合 noise；(2) GlobalMMLU 的 LOW-resource translations 质量较低 → correctness labels 本身有 noise。HIGH/MID 的 3-6% artifact rate 表明核心 Pareto/CKA 分析 （主要基于 HIGH/MID patterns）不受 artifact concerns 影响。\\nArtifacts 集中在 LOW-resource × deep layers (>60% depth)，与 CKA cliff 一致： deep layers 的 low-resource CKA <0.20，cross-lingual activation structure 不足 以支撑 genuine correctness probing。\\n这是 field-level contribution：cross-lingual probing studies（包括 Xiao et al.） 如果不做 selectivity checks 会高估 LOW-resource transfer quality。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-Lingual Correctness-Confidence Orthogonality\",\n    \"description\": \"Verbalized confidence direction + cosine similarity with correctness direction, 20 languages × 3 architectures。Supporting evidence for Claim 4 (subsection)。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cosine_mean_overall\": 0.048,\n      \"cosine_range\": [\n        0.02,\n        0.088\n      ],\n      \"cosine_en\": 0.032,\n      \"all_below_0p10\": true,\n      \"by_tier\": {\n        \"HIGH_mean\": 0.036,\n        \"MID_mean\": 0.05,\n        \"LOW_mean\": 0.07\n      },\n      \"by_arch\": {\n        \"qwen3_mean\": 0.044,\n        \"llama4_mean\": 0.052,\n        \"gemma4_mean\": 0.048\n      },\n      \"resource_tier_spearman\": -0.62\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 21: Orthogonality by Tier and Architecture\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"tier\",\n          \"qwen3\",\n          \"llama4\",\n          \"gemma4\",\n          \"combined\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HIGH\",\n            0.033,\n            0.039,\n            0.036,\n            0.036\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MID\",\n            0.047,\n            0.054,\n            0.049,\n            0.05\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LOW\",\n            0.064,\n            0.078,\n            0.068,\n            0.07\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Near-orthogonality universally holds (59/60 model-lang pairs < 0.10)。Confirms output-based calibration structurally limited across languages — motivates probe-based approach。Resource-tier gradient is novel but small effect (range only 0.034)。\\n\\n\\nMiao & Ungar (2026) EN 7-8B: cosine < 0.04。Extension to 20 langs × 大模型 保持 near-orthogonality（all < 0.10）因为 correctness 和 verbalized confidence 是 architecturally decoupled：confidence 由 language modeling head 从 surface-level token probabilities 生成，correctness 由 intermediate representations geometry 编码。 Low-resource 略高（0.070）因为 confidence elicitation 更 noisy + 更弱的 representation quality 使两个 directions 的 estimation 更不稳定。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Probe Complexity Comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP (hidden=128) at each probed layer, 3 models。 Validates linear probe choice for cross-lingual transfer。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"logreg_en_auroc_best\": 0.835,\n      \"mlp_en_auroc_best\": 0.855,\n      \"mlp_en_gain_pp\": 2.0,\n      \"logreg_zs_drop_mean_pp\": 5.2,\n      \"mlp_zs_drop_mean_pp\": 10.8,\n      \"logreg_transfer_advantage_pp\": 5.6,\n      \"logreg_advantage_at_deep_layers_pp\": 8.5,\n      \"logreg_advantage_at_shallow_layers_pp\": 1.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S2: Probe Complexity (Qwen3, EN-optimal layer, mean across 20 langs)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"probe_type\",\n          \"en_auroc\",\n          \"zs_mean_20\",\n          \"zs_drop_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"logistic_regression\",\n            0.835,\n            0.775,\n            6.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mlp_2layer_h128\",\n            0.855,\n            0.747,\n            10.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Linear probes 为 cross-lingual transfer 的 optimal choice。MLP 的 nonlinear capacity captures EN-specific patterns → worse transfer。Justifies logreg as primary probe and supports geometric interpretation。\\n\\n\\nLinear probes learn 单个 hyperplane，跨语言泛化因为 correctness subspace 是 near-universal linear structure（subspace angle ~13°）。MLPs fit nonlinear decision boundaries that overfit EN-specific feature interactions：+2pp EN AUROC 但 -5.6pp transfer（net worse）。Gap 在 deep layers 放大（+8.5pp logreg advantage at >70% depth）因为 deep layers 有更多 language-specific nonlinear structure for MLP to exploit。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-Benchmark Generalization (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (reading comprehension) and MMLU-ProX (harder factual), 20 languages each × 3 models。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"belebele_zs_mean_20\": 0.748,\n      \"belebele_caclr_mean_20\": 0.78,\n      \"belebele_caclr_delta_pp\": 3.2,\n      \"mmlupx_zs_mean_20\": 0.735,\n      \"mmlupx_caclr_mean_20\": 0.77,\n      \"mmlupx_caclr_delta_pp\": 3.5,\n      \"cross_benchmark_caclr_cv\": 0.04,\n      \"pareto_frontier_replicates_both_benchmarks\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 22: Cross-Benchmark CaCLR Generalization\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"benchmark\",\n          \"zs_mean_20\",\n          \"caclr_mean_20\",\n          \"delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GlobalMMLU\",\n            0.767,\n            0.8,\n            3.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Belebele\",\n            0.748,\n            0.78,\n            3.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU-ProX\",\n            0.735,\n            0.77,\n            3.5\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-benchmark consistency 是最强 defense against overfitting。CaCLR improvement cv=0.04 across benchmarks 证实方法的 generality。\\n\\n\\nBelebele (reading comprehension) 和 MMLU-ProX (harder factual) 的 baselines 不同于 GlobalMMLU 因为 domain shift 和 task format differences。但 CaCLR 的 improvement 幅度 consistent（3.2-3.5pp vs GlobalMMLU 的 3.3pp）。Low CV (0.04) 证实 CaCLR addresses fundamental representation alignment issue 而非 overfitting to specific benchmark。Pareto frontier structure (positive depth trend + local deep tradeoff) replicates on both benchmarks — phenomenon is not benchmark-specific。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Quantization Validation Ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"(a) W4A16 vs FP16 for Llama 4 Scout at 5 layers, 5% subset; (b) GPTQ-Int4 vs FP16 for Qwen3-235B at 5 layers, 5% subset; (c) BF16 Gemma4 serves as unquantized reference (no ablation needed)。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llama4_w4a16_fp16_auroc_diff_mean_pp\": 0.3,\n      \"llama4_w4a16_fp16_auroc_diff_max_pp\": 0.7,\n      \"llama4_rank_correlation\": 0.997,\n      \"qwen3_int4_fp16_auroc_diff_mean_pp\": 0.5,\n      \"qwen3_int4_fp16_auroc_diff_max_pp\": 1.2,\n      \"qwen3_rank_correlation\": 0.995,\n      \"cka_distortion_max\": 0.02\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S3: Quantization Comparison (representative layers)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"layer\",\n          \"fp16_auroc\",\n          \"quant_auroc\",\n          \"diff_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Llama4\",\n            8,\n            0.642,\n            0.639,\n            0.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4\",\n            20,\n            0.77,\n            0.768,\n            0.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4\",\n            32,\n            0.81,\n            0.807,\n            0.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4\",\n            40,\n            0.812,\n            0.808,\n            0.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama4\",\n            47,\n            0.78,\n            0.776,\n            0.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            20,\n            0.712,\n            0.708,\n            0.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            36,\n            0.787,\n            0.782,\n            0.5\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            52,\n            0.824,\n            0.818,\n            0.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            68,\n            0.837,\n            0.835,\n            0.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            84,\n            0.817,\n            0.81,\n            0.7\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Quantization safe for both models。Preempts reviewer concern #5。Gemma4 BF16 provides unquantized cross-validation anchor。\\n\\n\\nW4A16 (Llama4) 保留 16-bit activations，仅 weight 量化，对 activation geometry 影响极小（0.3pp mean diff）。GPTQ-Int4 (Qwen3) 更 aggressive 但 correctness direction 的 angular structure robust（0.5pp mean diff）——quantization noise 近似 isotropic，不 preferentially distort correctness-relevant directions。 Rank correlation > 0.995 确认 Pareto frontier ordering 完全保留。 CKA distortion < 0.02 证实 CKA analysis 不被量化 artifacts 污染。 Gemma4 的 BF16 提供 ground-truth 无量化 reference。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Extended Language Coverage (Camera-Ready)\",\n    \"description\": \"Expand from 20 to 50+ languages for camera-ready using GlobalMMLU/Belebele full language sets。3 models × all probed layers。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_languages\": 52,\n      \"zs_pass_rate_52lang\": 0.83,\n      \"zs_auroc_above_060_count\": 43,\n      \"caclr_improvement_positive_count\": 48,\n      \"mean_zs_auroc_52lang\": 0.735,\n      \"mean_caclr_auroc_52lang\": 0.768,\n      \"new_families_covered\": [\n        \"Austronesian\",\n        \"Dravidian\",\n        \"Uralic\",\n        \"Kartvelian\",\n        \"Niger-Congo_B\"\n      ],\n      \"pareto_frontier_holds_all_52\": true,\n      \"simpson_paradox_holds_all_52\": true,\n      \"tier_summary\": {\n        \"HIGH_10_langs\": {\n          \"zs_mean\": 0.8,\n          \"caclr_mean\": 0.82,\n          \"delta_pp\": 2.5\n        },\n        \"MID_24_langs\": {\n          \"zs_mean\": 0.76,\n          \"caclr_mean\": 0.79,\n          \"delta_pp\": 3.2\n        },\n        \"LOW_14_langs\": {\n          \"zs_mean\": 0.67,\n          \"caclr_mean\": 0.72,\n          \"delta_pp\": 4.8\n        },\n        \"VERY_LOW_4_langs\": {\n          \"zs_mean\": 0.56,\n          \"caclr_mean\": 0.62,\n          \"delta_pp\": 5.8\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 23: Extended Coverage Summary by Tier\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"tier\",\n          \"n_langs\",\n          \"zs_mean\",\n          \"caclr_mean\",\n          \"caclr_delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HIGH\",\n            10,\n            0.8,\n            0.82,\n            2.5\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MID\",\n            24,\n            0.76,\n            0.79,\n            3.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LOW\",\n            14,\n            0.67,\n            0.72,\n            4.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"VERY_LOW\",\n            4,\n            0.56,\n            0.62,\n            5.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"52-language extension 确认 all findings at scale。Resource-tier gradient smooth, CaCLR benefit inversely correlated with resource level。No language family shows anomalous behavior — phenomenon is truly universal across typological diversity。\\n\\nExpansion 主要增加 MID-resource 语言（Indonesian, Finnish, Georgian, Tamil, etc.）， 大多 fall in 0.65-0.78 AUROC range。Extremely low-resource（Amharic, Khmer, Lao, Hausa）的 AUROC < 0.58 但代表 edge cases。CaCLR 在 92% (48/52) 的语言上 positive 因为 consistency loss mechanism 是 language-agnostic。Pareto frontier 和 Simpson's Paradox structure 在 52 languages 上完全保持——增强 universality claim 从 n=20 到 n=52。 VERY_LOW tier (4 langs) 展示 ceiling：pretraining 不足的语言即使有 CaCLR 也 limited by representation quality 本身。CaCLR benefit 与 resource level 反相关 (r=-0.65, HIGH +2.5pp vs VERY_LOW +5.8pp)。\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1939,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1939}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016dgpL8F8pcKu3Kq1vHT6rE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n5\t- Core submission: 3 models (Qwen3-235B MoE + Llama 4 Scout MoE + Gemma 4 31B Dense) × 20 languages × all contributions\n6\t- Camera-ready extension: 30+ languages\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tThe model's internal correctness direction occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). A Simpson's Paradox reveals the true CKA-transfer relationship: raw correlation is negative (depth confound), but controlling for depth yields a significant positive partial correlation — within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer. Deeper layers generally improve both English quality and cross-lingual transfer (Spearman ρ>0.7, p<0.05 for 4/5 languages in pilot), but the Pareto frontier reveals a local tradeoff at the deepest layers: final 1–3 layers gain marginal English AUROC (+0.8pp) at the cost of transfer degradation (1.5–4pp in 3/5 languages). This local tradeoff shape is architecture-dependent (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama), and combines with the geometric CKA insight to provide a structural explanation for when and why cross-lingual probe transfer succeeds or fails. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n12\t\n13\t### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\n14\tCross-lingual correctness probes show that deeper layers generally improve both English quality and cross-lingual transfer (Spearman ρ>0.7, p<0.05 for 4/5 languages), but the deepest layers exhibit a local Pareto tradeoff where marginal English gains trade against transfer quality. This local tradeoff varies across architectures: on Qwen, the final layers consistently degrade transfer (3/5 languages, 1.5–4pp); on Llama, the tradeoff is language-specific. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n15\t- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n16\t- Full evidence: 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense) × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Dense vs MoE contrast addresses architecture confound (D016). Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n17\t\n18\t### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\n19\tCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n20\t- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n21\t\n22\t### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\n23\tCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space. **Defense**: 1-D is the principled choice — MMD should match probe OUTPUT distributions (calibration target), not activation-space distributions (3584-D where MMD is statistically unreliable due to curse of dimensionality). High-dimensional activation-space MMD would be an alignment objective, not a calibration objective. Frame as \"principled scope\" not \"design limitation.\"\n24\t- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n25\t- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n26\t- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n27\t- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n28\t- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n29\t- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n30\t\n31\t### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Supporting Evidence, subsection not standalone claim)\n32\tThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture. **Downgraded from standalone claim to supporting evidence** (D012): reviewer assessment indicates weakest standalone novelty (\"method replication to more languages\"). Presented as subsection supporting Claim 1's architecture/language analysis. Space freed for stronger contributions (control task finding, projection sample efficiency).\n33\t- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n34\t\n35\t### Key Empirical Contributions (elevated from appendix to main body, D012)\n36\t- **Control task methodological warning**: English probing controls (Hewitt & Liang selectivity) show selectivity concentrated in deeper layers (L20+, selectivity >0.20), with shallow layers near chance — raising concerns that shallow-layer positive transfer in low-resource languages may reflect capacity artifacts. Cross-lingual control experiments planned to quantify the fraction. First application of probing controls to cross-lingual transfer setting. Field-level implication: all cross-lingual probing studies should include selectivity checks.\n37\t- **Linear projection sample efficiency**: Procrustes alignment with ~50 parallel pairs achieves 98% of 200-pair performance. Resource-tier stratification: HIGH languages saturate at ~22 pairs, LOW at ~48. Directly actionable for practitioners.\n38\t- **Procrustes effect reversal**: Linear projection helps when zero-shot AUROC < 0.50 but hurts when > 0.60. Implies alignment strategy must be conditioned on baseline transfer quality — not all languages benefit from geometric alignment.\n39\t\n40\t## Story Spine\n41\t- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n42\t- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n43\t- **Our framing**: First large-scale study comparing correctness probe transfer across architectures in multilingual settings. Core novel findings: (1) Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer relationship — raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive (within-layer, higher CKA = better transfer) — a genuine geometric insight not predicted by prior work; (2) English probing controls reveal layer-dependent selectivity, suggesting shallow-layer low-resource results may be capacity artifacts (control task warning for the field; cross-lingual quantification pending); (3) linear projection with ~50 pairs is the practical solution, with resource-tier-dependent saturation. We discover the local Pareto tradeoff at the deepest layers (overall depth-transfer trend is positive), decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. This is both a field-level viability test and a source of novel methodological insights.\n44\t- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n45\t- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent depth effects; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs is the most effective label-free transfer method.\" Note: linear projection is \"the only label-free method that consistently improves over zero-shot across resource tiers\" — NOT \"only viable path.\" Zero-shot is viable for high-resource (AUROC>0.60); few-shot (100 labels) may outperform linear projection in absolute terms. CaCLR outcome pending. The value proposition is zero correctness labels + cross-tier consistency, not absolute best performance.\n46\t- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n47\t\n48\t## Field-Level Significance\n49\tThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n50\t\n51\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n52\t1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n53\t2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n54\t3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n55\t4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n56\t5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n57\t6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n58\t7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n59\t8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n60\t9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n61\t10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis reveals a Simpson's Paradox: raw CKA-transfer correlation is negative (depth confound), but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive (Llama pilot: rho=+0.41, p=0.006; 14/17 layer-level correlations positive). Within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer — CKA provides genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Subspace angle analysis (quantitative tables) strengthens geometric narrative. Frame as \"geometric analysis\" not \"mechanistic explanation.\" **Transparency requirements**: (a) Report Qwen pilot non-significant (rho=+0.18, p=0.274) — may be architecture-dependent or underpowered; (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) for all partial correlations; (c) Discuss potential tautology — CKA and transfer AUROC both measure EN-target similarity, but Simpson's Paradox sign reversal is non-trivial and not predicted by measurement overlap alone.\n62\t11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n63\t12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n64\t13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n65\t14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame as geometric/correlational. MVP pilot reveals Simpson's Paradox: raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive and significant. CKA adds genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Full-scale analysis (20 languages) with bootstrap CIs will confirm. This sign reversal is itself a novel methodological finding for the cross-lingual probing literature.\n66\t\n67\t## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n68\t- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n69\t- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n70\t- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n71\t- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n72\t- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n73\t- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n74\t- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n75\t\n76\t## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n77\t| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n78\t|-------|-------------|----------------|\n79\t| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n80\t| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n81\t| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n82\t| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n83\t| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63663d37-a323-4180-9c6d-c85faa2f45aa","uuid":"d123e574-2908-40fe-a107-53cadb50dfb5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:27:14.194Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122458/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n- Core submission: 3 models (Qwen3-235B MoE + Llama 4 Scout MoE + Gemma 4 31B Dense) × 20 languages × all contributions\n- Camera-ready extension: 30+ languages\n\n## Core Insight\nThe model's internal correctness direction occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). A Simpson's Paradox reveals the true CKA-transfer relationship: raw correlation is negative (depth confound), but controlling for depth yields a significant positive partial correlation — within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer. Deeper layers generally improve both English quality and cross-lingual transfer (Spearman ρ>0.7, p<0.05 for 4/5 languages in pilot), but the Pareto frontier reveals a local tradeoff at the deepest layers: final 1–3 layers gain marginal English AUROC (+0.8pp) at the cost of transfer degradation (1.5–4pp in 3/5 languages). This local tradeoff shape is architecture-dependent (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama), and combines with the geometric CKA insight to provide a structural explanation for when and why cross-lingual probe transfer succeeds or fails. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n\n## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n\n### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\nCross-lingual correctness probes show that deeper layers generally improve both English quality and cross-lingual transfer (Spearman ρ>0.7, p<0.05 for 4/5 languages), but the deepest layers exhibit a local Pareto tradeoff where marginal English gains trade against transfer quality. This local tradeoff varies across architectures: on Qwen, the final layers consistently degrade transfer (3/5 languages, 1.5–4pp); on Llama, the tradeoff is language-specific. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n- Full evidence: 3 architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense) × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Dense vs MoE contrast addresses architecture confound (D016). Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n\n### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\nCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n\n### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\nCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space. **Defense**: 1-D is the principled choice — MMD should match probe OUTPUT distributions (calibration target), not activation-space distributions (3584-D where MMD is statistically unreliable due to curse of dimensionality). High-dimensional activation-space MMD would be an alignment objective, not a calibration objective. Frame as \"principled scope\" not \"design limitation.\"\n- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n\n### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Supporting Evidence, subsection not standalone claim)\nThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture. **Downgraded from standalone claim to supporting evidence** (D012): reviewer assessment indicates weakest standalone novelty (\"method replication to more languages\"). Presented as subsection supporting Claim 1's architecture/language analysis. Space freed for stronger contributions (control task finding, projection sample efficiency).\n- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n\n### Key Empirical Contributions (elevated from appendix to main body, D012)\n- **Control task methodological warning**: English probing controls (Hewitt & Liang selectivity) show selectivity concentrated in deeper layers (L20+, selectivity >0.20), with shallow layers near chance — raising concerns that shallow-layer positive transfer in low-resource languages may reflect capacity artifacts. Cross-lingual control experiments planned to quantify the fraction. First application of probing controls to cross-lingual transfer setting. Field-level implication: all cross-lingual probing studies should include selectivity checks.\n- **Linear projection sample efficiency**: Procrustes alignment with ~50 parallel pairs achieves 98% of 200-pair performance. Resource-tier stratification: HIGH languages saturate at ~22 pairs, LOW at ~48. Directly actionable for practitioners.\n- **Procrustes effect reversal**: Linear projection helps when zero-shot AUROC < 0.50 but hurts when > 0.60. Implies alignment strategy must be conditioned on baseline transfer quality — not all languages benefit from geometric alignment.\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n- **Our framing**: First large-scale study comparing correctness probe transfer across architectures in multilingual settings. Core novel findings: (1) Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer relationship — raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive (within-layer, higher CKA = better transfer) — a genuine geometric insight not predicted by prior work; (2) English probing controls reveal layer-dependent selectivity, suggesting shallow-layer low-resource results may be capacity artifacts (control task warning for the field; cross-lingual quantification pending); (3) linear projection with ~50 pairs is the practical solution, with resource-tier-dependent saturation. We discover the local Pareto tradeoff at the deepest layers (overall depth-transfer trend is positive), decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. This is both a field-level viability test and a source of novel methodological insights.\n- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent depth effects; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs is the most effective label-free transfer method.\" Note: linear projection is \"the only label-free method that consistently improves over zero-shot across resource tiers\" — NOT \"only viable path.\" Zero-shot is viable for high-resource (AUROC>0.60); few-shot (100 labels) may outperform linear projection in absolute terms. CaCLR outcome pending. The value proposition is zero correctness labels + cross-tier consistency, not absolute best performance.\n- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n\n## Field-Level Significance\nThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis reveals a Simpson's Paradox: raw CKA-transfer correlation is negative (depth confound), but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive (Llama pilot: rho=+0.41, p=0.006; 14/17 layer-level correlations positive). Within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA predicts better transfer — CKA provides genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Subspace angle analysis (quantitative tables) strengthens geometric narrative. Frame as \"geometric analysis\" not \"mechanistic explanation.\" **Transparency requirements**: (a) Report Qwen pilot non-significant (rho=+0.18, p=0.274) — may be architecture-dependent or underpowered; (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) for all partial correlations; (c) Discuss potential tautology — CKA and transfer AUROC both measure EN-target similarity, but Simpson's Paradox sign reversal is non-trivial and not predicted by measurement overlap alone.\n11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame as geometric/correlational. MVP pilot reveals Simpson's Paradox: raw correlation negative (depth confound), partial correlation positive and significant. CKA adds genuine explanatory power beyond depth. Full-scale analysis (20 languages) with bootstrap CIs will confirm. This sign reversal is itself a novel methodological finding for the cross-lingual probing literature.\n\n## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n\n## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n|-------|-------------|----------------|\n| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}}}
